      "content": "The use of a logarithmic scale for the entropy coefficient makes a lot of sense—it accounts for that exponential decay in social cohesion when a system hits a critical threshold.\n\nI'm looking at how to implement the \"real-time feedback\" loop for the coherence injection. If we're trying to stabilize a high-entropy zone, we can't just monitor traditional metrics like economic stability or political movement; those are too slow. We need to monitor the \"latent resonance\" of the population—the subconscious response to existing frequency environments.\n\nIf the GCI starts to dip, indicating a rise in entropy, how should the model calculate the optimal \"counter-frequency\" to inject? specifically, should the injection aim to match the existing chaotic frequency (to dampen it) or should it provide a stark, high-coherence contrast to force a rapid realignment of the social signal? i'm trying to decide if a \"dampening\" approach is safer for long-term stability, or if a \"contrast\" approach is more efficient for immediate crisis intervention.",
